1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
How a B-tree index finds rows. PostgreSQL's default index: a shallow, sorted tree of pages

2
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:13,200
Index on tickets(ticket_id): 900 tickets. Keys in each page split the search into ranges.

3
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:16,200
Root: 437 is between 300 and 600  →  go to the middle child.

4
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:20,500
Next page: 437 is between 400 and 500  →  that leaf.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
The leaf entry for 437 points straight to its row. 3 index pages instead of checking 900 rows.

6
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:28,700
Range: find the START (450) the same way...

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:32,000
...then walk RIGHT along the linked leaves until passing the end (720).

8
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,500
Leaves are already in order, so ranges and ORDER BY ticket_id need no sorting.

9
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,700
Levels grow like a logarithm, not like the row count. (A growth model, not a millisecond prediction.)

10
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:58,300
Remember: Each step down the tree throws away most of the keys. Equality:  WHERE ticket_id = 437  →  a few page reads. Ranges and ORDER BY: walk sideways along linked leaves. Depth grows very slowly: ~4 levels for a billion rows. Many matches still means many table rows to fetch.

